NM(1)                       User Commands                    NM(1)

NAME
    nm - list the symbols of object files

SYNOPSIS
    nm [-aABDfgjnoPprSsuUW] [-t radix] [-f format] [--size-sort] [file...]

DESCRIPTION
    nm lists the symbols of ELF64 objects, shared objects, programs and
    archives of them. The default file is a.out. The output matches GNU nm.
    Each symbol line shows the value, a type letter and the name. Upper
    case letters mark global symbols and lower case letters mark local
    symbols.

TYPE LETTERS
    A   absolute value
    B   symbol in a section without contents (bss)
    C   common symbol
    D   symbol in a writable data section
    i   indirect function
    N   symbol in a debugging section
    n   symbol in a read-only section that is not loaded
    R   symbol in a read-only data section
    T   symbol in a code section
    U   undefined symbol
    u   unique global symbol
    V   weak object, W weak symbol; v and w mark undefined weak symbols
    ?   symbol of unknown kind

OPTIONS
    -a, --debug-syms        list section and file symbols
    -A, -o, --print-file-name
                            print the file before every symbol
    -B                      same as --format=bsd
    -D, --dynamic           list the dynamic symbols
    -f, --format=FORMAT     bsd (default), sysv, posix or just-symbols
    -g, --extern-only       list global symbols only
    -j, --just-symbols      same as --format=just-symbols
    -n, -v, --numeric-sort  sort by address
    -p, --no-sort           use the order of the symbol table
    -P, --portability       same as --format=posix
    -r, --reverse-sort      reverse the sort order
    -S, --print-size        print the size of defined symbols
    -s, --print-armap       print the symbol index of archives
    --size-sort             sort by size
    -t, --radix=RADIX       print values in radix d, o or x
    -u, --undefined-only    list undefined symbols only
    -U, --defined-only      list defined symbols only
    -W, --no-weak           omit weak symbols
    --special-syms          include mapping symbols of aarch64
    --quiet                 omit the message for files without symbols

SEE ALSO
    size(1), strings(1), ar(1), readelf(1)
